DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as Particl
 e Pollution Levels Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges
DESCRIPTION:Maine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as Particle Pollution Level
 s Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges\nMaine Department of Environme
 ntal Protection sent this bulletin at 07/17/2026 03:23 PM EDT\n--- PRESS R
 ELEASE ---\nFor Immediate Release\nMaine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as P
 article Pollution Levels Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges\nAUGUST
 A\, July 17\, 2026\n— The Maine Department of Environmental Protection w
 arns that ground-level particle pollution concentrations are forecasted to
  reach the ‘Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups’ (USG) level this Saturday 
 due to wildfire smoke drifting in from Manitoba and Ontario\, Canada.\nThe
  smoke plume is expected to move back over Maine during the day on Saturda
 y\, with air quality measured by a 24-hour average forecasted at the USG l
 evel. Some hours may experience particle pollution levels even higher than
  this range. Residents are encouraged to follow health recommendations tha
 t suit their individual needs.\nSmoke conditions may improve by midday Sun
 day\, but due to the unpredictable nature of wildfire smoke\, the Maine De
 partment of Environmental Protection advises checking their website or cal
 ling the toll-free Air Quality Hotline at 800-223-1196 for the latest upda
 tes throughout the weekend.\nElevated particle pollution levels can affect
  sensitive groups such as children\, older adults\, outdoor workers\, and 
 individuals with respiratory or heart conditions including asthma\, bronch
 itis\, and COPD. Symptoms may include coughing\, shortness of breath\, thr
 oat irritation\, and mild chest pain. Even healthy adults engaging in outd
 oor physical activity might notice these effects.\nSome actions you can ta
 ke to protect your health during such periods include:\nAvoiding strenuous
  outdoor activity\nClosing windows and circulating indoor air with a fan o
 r air conditioner\nAsthmatics should keep their quick-relief medications a
 nd action plan handy\nAdditional health information may be found on the fo
 llowing websites:\nMaine CDC asthma prevention an\nhttps://content.govdeli
 very.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/420fa43
URL:https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/420fa43
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
